WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.500
- [Narrator] These little
creatures can be complicated.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.560
They're cute, but mischievous.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.790
It's my pizza.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.850
They may have earned a
bad rap from their antics,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:16.590
but their problem solving skills
inspire scientific studies.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.830
Interactions with them are unpredictable,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.590
and as our cities spread into their homes,

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.390
that's only increasing.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.993
So, let's take a closer look at raccoons.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.540
- I think I have one of
the best jobs in the world.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.690
I am endlessly fascinated by animals,

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.570
in particular, animal
cognition and animal behavior.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.930
- [Narrator] This is Dr. Sarah
Benson-Amram, and she works

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.480
with some pretty great office mates.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.020
- I find it a really fun
challenge to try to put myself

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.670
into the minds of these
animals and come up

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.610
with a task that I think
is going to be fair

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.380
and actually get at the cognitive ability

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.180
that I'm trying to assess.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.560
Every time we test the raccoons,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.930
it just ends up leading
to 20 more questions.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.680
You could study them for a
lifetime and never get bored

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.170
- [Narrator] Smart, adaptable

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.083
and carrying a famous
resemblance around the eyes,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.670
Native American folklore
tells of a muddy brawl

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.280
with a coyote that caused the face mask,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.990
and their know-it-all antics,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.323
from being the reincarnation
of a disobedient human.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.360
Whether past or present,
they leave an impression.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.280
- Raccoons definitely have a reputation

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.830
for being mischievous,
and they're very good

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.870
at doing things that we
can find pretty frustrating

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.440
like breaking into trash cans
or getting into your house.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.606
- [Narrator] But to be fair,

00:01:38.606 --> 00:01:40.590
(chainsaw runs)

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.633
we're kind of breaking into theirs.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.690
- We're taking over more natural land

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:46.920
and more wildlife are getting pushed

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.440
into human dominated landscapes.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.840
- [Narrator] And we're
talking a lot of landscapes,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.670
like 24 million acres

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.700
of natural space lost to urban expansion.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.403
That's the size of nine Grand Canyons.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:01.122
- [Dr. Sarah Benson-Amram]
You have a lot of wildlife

00:02:01.122 --> 00:02:02.490
that is declining

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.950
and even disappearing from some areas,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.080
but then you also have
these other wildlife species

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.580
that are able to successfully live

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.723
in cities and sometimes even
use human provided resources.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.800
- [Narrator] Which brings us
back to our crafty neighbors.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.370
- Raccoons are really adept
at changing their behavior,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.320
but they're not avoiding people as much

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.690
as they're taking advantage
of a lot of the resources.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.330
- [Narrator] Despite how hard
we try to keep them away.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.900
- We put fences, we put bungee
cords on our trash cans.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.170
So we're basically
presenting urban wildlife

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.570
with increasingly difficult challenges,

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.850
and they're solving those challenges.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.890
- [Narrator] Which raises the question,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.933
just how smart are these animals?

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.900
- We've given them
different cognitive tasks

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.580
to specifically assess this ability

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.980
of theirs to change
their behavior rapidly.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.290
- [Narrator] One test
studies cognitive flexibility

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.330
with a three part puzzle.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.753
Another looks at reversal
learning by pressing buttons.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.310
But it's a fable from a
couple thousand years ago

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.140
that inspires the third.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.850
- The Aesop's fable paradigm is taken

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.370
from the Aesop's fable,
"The Crow &amp; the Pitcher".

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.130
There's a thirsty crow and
it finds a pitcher of water.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.060
There's only a little bit of water

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.800
in that pitcher and the
crow can't reach it,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.440
so this Crow drops stones into the pitcher

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.970
which raises the water level,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.830
now that the crow can get a drink.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.510
- [Narrator] And in real life, birds

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:25.110
like rooks and crows can
actually solve this problem,

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.620
as well as another animal
you might recognize.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.090
- We found some really
interesting differences

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.860
between what we see with raccoons

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.210
and what they see in other studies.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.850
Unlike the Corvids, they
don't just take one stone,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.890
drop it in, get the reward, and finish.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.680
They actually take all the
stones, dump them all in,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.260
and just are playing with
this thing constantly.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.180
So they get the reward, but
then they just keep playing

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.250
with it or they take the
stones and they go hide them

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:50.000
in their bedding so that they
can play with them later.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.410
Once the raccoons learn this,

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.750
you can test them to see
really how much they understand

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:55.583
about the problem.

00:03:55.583 --> 00:03:57.720
For example, you can
give them a floating ball

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.280
versus a sinking ball.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.560
If they understand they
should choose the sinking ball

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.450
because the floating ball
won't actually help them

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.620
to get their reward.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.020
They actually didn't show a
preference for the sinking ball.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:08.220
What they've figured out is

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.800
that they could actually
make the floating ball work.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.730
They learned to push it up

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.230
and down in the water to bounce it,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.420
and that would spray marshmallow bits

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.460
up the side of the tube so
they could get the reward.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.293
So we're seeing

00:04:18.293 --> 00:04:20.343
that they're really
creative problem solvers.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.440
- [Narrator] So for those
skilled problem solvers,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.810
what can we learn from their brains?

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.240
- Raccoons, who can solve
a problem in multiple ways,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.820
have more cells in their hippocampus,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.020
and the hippocampus is thought

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.020
to be this really important
brain region for cognition,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.150
specifically memory and learning.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.260
Interestingly it's actually

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.240
that they have more non-neuronal cells,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.100
so things like glia.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.770
- [Narrator] Which is helpful,
considering a higher ratio

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.400
of glia to neurons could
increase neuroplasticity,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.007
which is your ability to
flexibly switch your behavior.

00:04:54.007 --> 00:04:54.840
- [Dr. Sarah Benson-Amram]
Albert Einstein,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.030
notably, had a really high glia

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.620
to neuron ratio and humans
actually have a higher glia

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.110
to neuron ratio than other primates.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:04.943
- [Narrator] So we're learning

00:05:04.943 --> 00:05:07.110
that raccoon brain power plays a role

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.733
in their cognitive flexibility,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.780
but there's another physical
feature that contributes

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.701
to their craftiness.

00:05:14.701 --> 00:05:16.050
- [Dr. Sarah Benson-Amram]
Raccoons are very famous

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:18.210
for their dextrous forepaws.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.570
So they have these five
long fingers like primates.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.220
- [Narrator] Just
without opposable thumbs.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.393
- Hey.

00:05:24.393 --> 00:05:25.350
- [Dr. Sarah Benson-Amram]
Instead they can use sort

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:26.580
of both forepaws together

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.620
to lift and then manipulate objects.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.176
- [Narrator] And while this
manipulation skill is handy.

00:05:32.176 --> 00:05:33.009
(Audience groans)

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:33.842
All right.

00:05:33.842 --> 00:05:34.680
All right.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.480
It's not their only feature.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.540
- Their sense of touch
is incredibly sensitive.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.990
Evolutionarily raccoons
were trying to find food

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.260
in really murky water or in
the dirt underneath the water.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:47.970
And so they're digging around,

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.830
but they're not able to
see what they're doing,

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.480
and so they're really heavily relying

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.790
on this tactile ability.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.920
- [Narrator] Which explains
another endearing trait

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.993
they've held onto.

00:05:58.837 --> 00:06:00.467
- [Audience Member] Come on.

00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:01.860
- People think, "Oh, when
raccoons put something in water,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.645
they're washing it,"

00:06:03.645 --> 00:06:06.030
but actually when they
put something in water,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.620
their tactile sense is heightened

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.423
to feel things even more finely.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.070
As urban environments start expanding

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.020
that can have a lot of
really dramatic consequences

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:16.853
for wildlife.

00:06:17.712 --> 00:06:18.900
Designing mitigation strategies

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.140
for human wildlife conflict
will be really critical.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.250
- [Narrator] For some cities,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.390
ecological restoration has
already shown promising results.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.840
By boosting biodiversity,
reducing city pollution.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.940
and connecting people with nature.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.593
- There is this effect

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:36.840
that when people connect to
nature in their backyards

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.240
then they connect to
nature even more globally.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.010
They're more likely to be involved

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.930
in conservation efforts around the world,

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.000
and so that's something
that we're really excited

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.280
about to help people to get involved

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.990
in community science projects

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.720
and to really get people
excited about what's happening

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.453
in their city, in their
backyard, with wildlife.

